course: dictionary
lesson: 4968
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words wear more than one coat. «Inclined» can mean likely, sloping, or talented. The words around it show you which meaning it has, so read the sentence first.
explain: «I'm inclined to agree with you.» Here inclined to means likely to agree, or willing to agree. Test it by swapping in «willing to» and checking that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «The ramp is an inclined plane.» Now inclined means sloping, leaning at an angle. Compare «She is musically inclined», which means she has a natural talent for music. Same word, three different jobs.
fact: Inclined to do something means likely to do it, or wanting to do it.
fact: Inclined can mean sloping or leaning at an angle, as in «an inclined plane».
fact: An inclined plane is a ramp, one of the simple machines used in science.
fact: Inclined can mean having a natural talent, as in «mathematically inclined».
fact: Inclined is also the past form of incline, meaning leaned or bent.
exercise: «I'm inclined to agree with you.» What does inclined mean here? | Very tired | *Likely to do it, or willing to | Sloping up a hill | Bent over a desk
exercise: «The ramp is an inclined plane.» What does inclined mean in this sentence? | Very quick and fast | Hidden under the bed | *Sloping, leaning at an angle
exercise: «She is mathematically inclined.» What does this suggest about her? | She is leaning on the desk | She is feeling ill | She hates numbers | *She has a natural talent for maths
test: «He was inclined to be late every morning.» What does inclined mean here? | Leaned against the wall | Bored and tired | *Likely to be late | Sloped steeply
test: «Yesterday the tall tree inclined towards the river.» What does inclined mean here? | Turned into stone | Flew away in the wind | Grew very tall quickly | *Leaned or bent
exam: Which sentence uses inclined to mean sloping? | «She is musically inclined.» | *«The garden path is inclined steeply down to the lake.» | «Are you inclined to help me?» | «I'm inclined to think so.»
